#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158230} :activity/id #uuid "44aff9fc-2508-51a9-baf1-3f855c1f0a4c" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050606 :topic/id #uuid "57fe5849-4b30-59b2-99bd-4fb2b776d659" :topic/math-academy-id 2980} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158230 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158531 :tutorial/content "In statistics, we often want to use sample data to deduce (or **infer**) the values of various **population parameters**. The two most common population parameters that we wish to find are\n\n- the **population mean** $\\mu$, and\n- the **population variance** $\\sigma^2$.\n\nThe population mean is the mean of the *entire* population. Likewise, the population variance is the variance of the *entire* population. We should consider these values as *fixed* unknowns.\n\nSuppose, for example, we want to find the mean number of pages per book at a particular library. In other words, we wish to find the population mean $\\mu$. However, it is impractical to count the pages in every book. So, instead, we might approximate $\\mu$ using the following sampling procedure:\n\n1. Randomly choose, say, $5$ books off the shelf.\n2. Count the number of pages in each book.\n3. Compute the mean of the $5$ observations.\n\nIn this case, the **sample size** $n=5$.\n\nSuppose we follow this procedure and get the following **observations**:\n\n$$\n201, \\qquad 506, \\qquad 58, \\qquad 104, \\qquad 99\n$$\n\nIt's convenient to denote the individual observations as $x_i$. So, in this case, we have\n\n$$\nx_1 = 201,\\qquad x_2 = 506,\\qquad \\ldots,\\qquad x_5 = 99\n$$\n\nWe can compute the mean $\\overline{x}$ of our sample as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^(―) &= \\frac{1}{n}∑_(i = 1)^(n)x_{i} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{5}(201 + 506 + 58 + 104 + 99) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{5} \\cdot 968 \\\\\n&= 193.6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe value\n\n$$\n\\bar{x} = 193.6\n$$\n\nis called an **estimate** of the population mean $\\mu$. We sometimes write\n\n$$\n\\hat{\\mu} = 193.6\n$$\n\nto denote that the value $193.6$ is an estimate of $\\mu$.\n\nIt's important to realize that our estimate $\\bar{x}$ depends entirely on this particular sample. We'd get a different estimate for $\\mu$ if we took a different sample." :tutorial/id #uuid "359539bd-254d-5083-aa76-577b8a159438" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4738 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "fab3a24b-2709-54fe-985c-5fe95ec0712c" :step/math-academy-id 29343 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158287}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158231 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157355 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157172 :question/id #uuid "a485378b-0bce-5576-bcbc-2ef77df2da17" :question/math-academy-id "e-7882"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b60ce582-4127-57b8-bba8-04cf17169ca3" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050862}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157964} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157965}] :knowledge-point/title "Estimating a Population Mean From the Sample Mean"} :step/id #uuid "a21dbb27-5fb2-54ba-a76f-f7231e67b5fd" :step/math-academy-id 29347 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158288}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158287 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158560 :tutorial/content "It is helpful to think of the observations of a particular sample\n\n$$\nx_1,\\quad x_2,\\quad \\ldots,\\quad x_n\n$$\n\nas a particular instance of data generated by a sequence of random variables\n\n$$\nX_1,\\quad X_2,\\quad \\ldots,\\quad X_n\n$$\n\nLet's make sure we're clear about the notation:\n\n- $X_i$ is a *random variable* that denotes the $i$th observation in a random sample. Its value changes when we conduct a new sample.\n- $x_i$ is the value of the $i$th observation in a particular sample.\n\nWe typically assume that our sequence of random variables $X_1, X_2,\\ldots, X_n$ has the following properties:\n\n- They are **independent and identically distributed** (or **I.I.D.**), meaning they are independent and have the same probability distribution. Moreover, they have the same distribution as the population.\n- Their mean is equal to the population mean:\n$\\textrm{E}(X_i) = \\mu, \\qquad 1\\leq i\\leq n$\n- Their variance is equal to the population variance:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}(X_i) = \\sigma^2, \\qquad 1\\leq i\\leq n\n$$\n\nWe often refer to a sequence of random variables $X_1, X_2,\\ldots, X_n$ with these properties as a **random sample of size** $\\boldsymbol{n}$.\n\nIn general, if $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$, then the **sample mean** is given by\n\n$$\n\\overline{X} = \\dfrac1n\\sum_{i=1}^n X_i\n$$\n\nWe say that the sample mean $\\overline{X}$ is an **estimator** of the population mean $\\mu$.\n\nSince $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_n$ are random variables, so is $\\overline{X}$. The probability distribution of $\\overline{X}$ is called the **sampling distribution** of $\\overline{X}$. The sampling distribution of $\\overline{X}$ is an important topic we'll discuss in detail in a future lesson." :tutorial/id #uuid "b79ae73d-8ebe-5dda-916d-6eeb61ce850d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6297 :tutorial/title "The Sample Mean"} :step/id #uuid "c4fadf35-c701-59e2-9c8b-d385cd3565ae" :step/math-academy-id 38136 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158231}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158288 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158561 :tutorial/content "One important feature of $\\overline{X}$ is that it's an **unbiased estimator** of $\\mu$. This means that the expected value of $\\overline{X}$ equals $\\mu$, that is\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[\\overline{X}] = \\mu\n$$\n\nWe can easily show that $\\overline{X}$ is an unbiased estimator of $\\mu$ using the properties of expectation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[X^(―)] &= E[\\frac{1}{n}∑_(i = 1)^(n)X_{i}] \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{n} \\cdot E[∑_(i = 1)^(n)X_{i}] \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{n} \\cdot E[X_{1} + X_{2} + ⋯ + X_{n}] \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{n} \\cdot (E[X_{1}] + E[X_{2}] + ⋯ + E[X_{n}]) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{n} \\cdot (μ + μ + ⋯ + μ) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{n} \\cdot nμ \\\\\n&= μ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe fact that $\\overline{X}$ is an unbiased estimator of $\\mu$ is a desirable property. We'll meet so-called **biased estimators** in future lessons.\n\nFinally, any estimate $\\bar{x}$ of the population mean $\\mu$ found by computing the mean of a sample is called an **unbiased estimate** of $\\mu$." :tutorial/id #uuid "4c9ca398-f024-59cf-b484-80cf2f43cc8c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6298 :tutorial/title "The Sample Mean as an Unbiased Estimator"} :step/id #uuid "5d85b5ed-e218-547b-81cf-36a60c612b6f" :step/math-academy-id 38137 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158289}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158289 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157392 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186156993 :question/id #uuid "33bedb30-2c99-52e8-a665-e3bddeed7e13" :question/math-academy-id "e-10114"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "38455e74-50c2-5581-b9e1-a8dd0c85f826" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048317} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048508}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157598} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157599}] :knowledge-point/title "The Expected Value of the Sample Mean"} :step/id #uuid "2edb1e12-aed2-5310-a098-962a8588a4f0" :step/math-academy-id 38142}] :activity/title "The Sample Mean" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186157528}]}
